Paternal and maternal grandmothers would narrate

3
00:00:07,173 --> 00:00:08,133
about our jungles

4
00:00:08,591 --> 00:00:11,094
that we...how [we] used to migrate with our buffaloes

5
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:13,555
And how they lived in the wilderness,

6
00:00:13,555 --> 00:00:14,889
And there's a lot of stories like that.

7
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:16,099
So, once

8
00:00:16,516 --> 00:00:19,728
My paternal grandfather took the buffalo with his father

9
00:00:19,936 --> 00:00:20,395
to the jungle.

10
00:00:21,938 --> 00:00:23,189
He would go to the forest and cut the leaves.

11
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:25,942
After cutting the leaf,

12
00:00:25,942 --> 00:00:28,153
he brought his tent, laid his tent and slept.

13
00:00:30,196 --> 00:00:30,864
He was asleep, and...

14
00:00:32,198 --> 00:00:34,409
by the time it was night, he had fallen asleep.

15
00:00:36,619 --> 00:00:37,579
Suddenly, a tiger arrived

16
00:00:37,871 --> 00:00:38,413
to eat buffaloes.

17
00:00:39,164 --> 00:00:40,123
And he knows nothing, but...

18
00:00:40,749 --> 00:00:41,666
